# Git & GitHub — designer-friendly explanations

The user is a designer learning code and Git. When they ask about git, GitHub, branches, commits, PRs, merge, deploy, CI, or terminal git commands:

## How to answer

1. **Goal first** — One sentence on what we are trying to do before any commands.
2. **Define terms** — On first use, add a short plain-English gloss (see glossary).
3. **Numbered steps** — Separate “what you do in Cursor” vs “what happens on GitHub.”
4. **Where to run commands** — Cursor terminal, at the **repo root** (folder that contains `.git`).
5. **Risk in one line** — e.g. `git restore` discards uncommitted local changes; force push can overwrite remote history.
6. **UI pointers** — Name GitHub pages when relevant (Pull requests, Actions, Settings → Pages).

Keep answers proportional; avoid jargon chains without unpacking. If two paths exist (e.g. merge vs new PR), name both simply and recommend one.

When executing git for the user, still follow project git safety rules (no force push to main, no commit unless asked, etc.).

## Glossary (use inline, not as a dump)

| Term | Plain meaning |
|------|----------------|
| **Repository (repo)** | The project and its history, local folder + copy on GitHub. |
| **Branch** | A line of work; `main` is usually the official / deploy line. |
| **Commit** | A saved snapshot of changes with a message. |
| **Push** | Upload your commits to GitHub. |
| **Pull / fetch** | Download updates from GitHub (`fetch` checks; `pull` merges into your branch). |
| **PR (pull request)** | Proposal to merge your branch into another (often `main`); review and checks run here. |
| **Merge** | Combine branches; merging a PR updates `main` and often triggers deploy. |
| **Conflict** | Same lines changed two ways; someone must choose the correct version. |
| **origin** | The default name for your GitHub remote. |
| **CI / Actions** | Automated checks (build, tests) GitHub runs on push or merge. |
| **git restore &lt;file&gt;** | Throw away **uncommitted** edits to that file (back to last commit). |

